meta {
  name: Upload Quote Document
  type: http
  seq: 3
}

put {
  url: {{quoteDocumentUploadUrl}}
  body: text
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/pdf
}

body:text {
  %PDF-1.4
  1 0 obj<</Type/Catalog>>endobj
  trailer<</Root 1 0 R>>
  %%EOF
}

tests {
  test("storage accepts the upload", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });
}

docs {
  # PUT <uploadUrl> — the raw upload (step 2 of 3, PAC-39)

  Not an API endpoint: this goes **straight to object storage** (MinIO locally)
  using the URL from `Presign Quote Document`. It exists in the collection so the
  chain is runnable end-to-end and so the flow is documented where people look
  for it.

  - **Auth:** `none` — deliberately. The collection-level bearer would be sent on
    this request and **break the S3 signature**; a presigned URL carries its own
    credentials. This is the single easiest thing to get wrong in the flow, and
    the web client uses a bare `fetch` here rather than `apiFetch` for exactly
    this reason.

  ## Headers
  `Content-Type` **must** match the value that was signed (the
  `requiredHeaders` from the presign response), or storage rejects the PUT.

  ## Body
  A minimal valid PDF stub. The bytes are irrelevant to the API — only the
  object's **type and size**, which `POST /quote-recaps` reads back from storage
  via `HeadObject`. Because the server derives the size rather than trusting the
  declared one, this stub can be far smaller than the `size` sent at presign time
  and the chain still works.

  ## Response
  - `200 OK` — object stored; the key is now attachable.
  - `403 Forbidden` — signature mismatch. Almost always either an
    `Authorization` header being sent, a `Content-Type` that differs from the
    signed one, or an expired URL (the default TTL is 300s).
}
